    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my security+ and im kinda stuck on one thing. if we are already hashing passwords, why do we even bother with salting them? like wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep them safe?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a common misconception. While hashing passwords alone can provide basic security, it's still vulnerable to rainbow table attacks and brute-force attacks. Salting adds a random value to the password before hashing, making it more difficult for attackers to use precomputed tables of hashes (rainbow tables) to crack the passwords. This significantly increases the computational complexity and makes it a much stronger security measure.",
      "source": "target",
