    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense, but can u explain the rainbow table thing better? like how exactly does the salt break those tables? i'm having trouble visualizing how the math actually changes things.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rainbow tables are precomputed tables of hashes for common passwords, which attackers can use to crack your password. These tables typically store the hash of a pre-chosen password (e.g., common passwords like \"qwerty\") along with its corresponding plaintext (the original password). By adding a salt to your password before hashing, the resulting hash becomes different for the same password, making it incompatible with precomputed tables, as the hash format will be unfamiliar.",
      "source": "target",
